WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.880
<v Maya>Welcome back to part two of the Fitness Testing series.

2
00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:10.750
<v Maya>Last time we covered why we test, the objectives, and health screening with the PARQ and informed consent.

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.130
<v Dev>So the client is screened and signed.

4
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.290
<v Dev>Now we can finally test.

5
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.280
<v Maya>Almost.

6
00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.280
<v Maya>Today we answer two questions.

7
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.930
<v Maya>In what order do we run the tests, and why?

8
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.370
<v Maya>And then we do the very first test, blood pressure.

9
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.960
<v Maya>There are five components of fitness, and several tests for each.

10
00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.210
<v Maya>Which should come first and which last?

11
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:33.830
<v Maya>The order matters because it decides whether your results are valid and reliable.

12
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.350
<v Dev>Give me the list.

13
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.060
<v Maya>After the screening pre-step, it goes like this.

14
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.870
<v Maya>Step one, blood pressure.

15
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.050
<v Maya>Step two, body composition.

16
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.020
<v Maya>Step three, cardiorespiratory endurance.

17
00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.110
<v Maya>Step four, muscular strength.

18
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.500
<v Maya>Step five, muscular endurance.

19
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.560
<v Maya>Step six, flexibility.

20
00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.020
<v Dev>Is that a ranking?

21
00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.360
<v Dev>Is blood pressure most important?

22
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.200
<v Maya>No.

23
00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:01.000
<v Maya>Steps two to six are the five components, and step one is an extra health marker.

24
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.280
<v Maya>The numbers are an order, not a rank.

25
00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.770
<v Maya>All components are equally important.

26
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.520
<v Maya>The order just gives the most accurate result for every test.

27
00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:10.520
<v Dev>Fine.

28
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.690
<v Dev>Why is blood pressure first?

29
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.110
<v Maya>The PARQ and consent describe the client's history over the last three or six months.

30
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.580
<v Maya>But you also need the client's condition on the day of testing, especially at the first test, when you know nothing about them.

31
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:31.950
<v Maya>If blood pressure is already high and the client never mentioned it, physical activity could push it higher and things could go out of control.

32
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.100
<v Maya>So it must be normal at the start.

33
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.630
<v Dev>And if it's high?

34
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:36.820
<v Maya>Four steps.

35
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.790
<v Maya>One, sit the client down in a calm, relaxed place and offer water.

36
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.080
<v Maya>Two, check again after some time.

37
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.080
<v Maya>If it is back to normal, begin.

38
00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:50.160
<v Maya>Three, if it stays high, skip the test that day and try again on the second and third day.

39
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:58.300
<v Maya>Four, if it is high on all three days, it can indicate a high blood pressure condition, and the client should see a doctor if they haven't already.

40
00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.960
<v Dev>Why three days?

41
00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:04.990
<v Maya>One high reading may be due to a stressful day or activity before coming to the gym.

42
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.050
<v Maya>You want to be sure the testing day is a normal day.

43
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.270
<v Dev>Next one, body composition.

44
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.680
<v Dev>Why second?

45
00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.870
<v Maya>Body composition is the ratio of body fat and lean body mass.

46
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:26.470
<v Maya>Body weight splits into lean body mass, which is muscles, organs, bones, fluids and essential fat, and body fat, which is visceral fat and subcutaneous fat.

47
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.800
<v Maya>A memory aid for lean mass is M, O, B, F and EF.

48
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.670
<v Dev>And why test it early?

49
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.560
<v Maya>Body weight changes before and after a workout.

50
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.660
<v Maya>Sweating causes fluid loss and salt depletion.

51
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:42.560
<v Maya>To get an accurate reading, the body must be neutral, nothing changed.

52
00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:48.400
<v Maya>That is why the popular BIA machine is done first thing in the morning before eating or drinking.

53
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.910
<v Dev>Third, cardio.

54
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.700
<v Dev>Why before strength?

55
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.230
<v Maya>In cardio, the key measure is heart rate.

56
00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:02.480
<v Maya>Imagine a client does ten squats, ten push-ups and ten minutes of jogging, and then you find the heart rate is, hypothetically, 132.

57
00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.470
<v Maya>How much came from the jogging?

58
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:05.660
<v Maya>You can't say.

59
00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:09.460
<v Maya>The rise should be solely the contribution of the cardio task.

60
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:13.740
<v Maya>Strength tests, endurance tests and even stretching raise heart rate.

61
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.730
<v Maya>So cardio goes before all of them.

62
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.910
<v Maya>Here's the one most people get wrong.

63
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.310
<v Maya>Strength or endurance first, Dev?

64
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.510
<v Dev>Endurance.

65
00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.580
<v Dev>Warm up the muscles first?

66
00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.240
<v Maya>Many people say that.

67
00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.430
<v Maya>The correct answer is strength first.

68
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.860
<v Maya>Let me define them.

69
00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:35.790
<v Maya>Muscular strength is the ability of the working skeletal muscles to generate maximum force in one single effort.

70
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:42.140
<v Maya>Muscular endurance is the ability to contract submaximally for a long time without getting fatigued.

71
00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:43.980
<v Dev>So why strength first?

72
00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:45.160
<v Maya>Two reasons.

73
00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:46.770
<v Maya>Reason one, fatigue.

74
00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:52.290
<v Maya>A bench press tests strength and push-ups test endurance, both using the pectoralis major.

75
00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.290
<v Maya>If you do push-ups first, the muscle is fatigued, and then you expect maximum force.

76
00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.570
<v Maya>That is unfair.

77
00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.420
<v Maya>The muscle must be fresh.

78
00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.000
<v Dev>And reason two?

79
00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.250
<v Maya>The priming or relativity effect.

80
00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.600
<v Maya>Think about a car going at 100 kilometres per hour.

81
00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:08.830
<v Maya>Feels fast.

82
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.160
<v Maya>Now sit in a very fast car at 200, then come back to 100.

83
00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.150
<v Maya>It no longer feels that fast.

84
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:22.620
<v Maya>A muscle that has done a one repetition maximum test, the 200, finds the push-up test, the 100, less challenging.

85
00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.040
<v Maya>So the client may do more push-ups than they would have done first.

86
00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.630
<v Dev>So strength first helps both tests.

87
00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:29.520
<v Maya>Yes.

88
00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.270
<v Maya>Last is flexibility.

89
00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.280
<v Maya>Flexibility is the ability of soft tissues to lengthen passively.

90
00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:38.820
<v Maya>And tissue temperature matters, warmer tissues extend better.

91
00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.950
<v Maya>If you test flexibility early, the tissues are cold.

92
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.540
<v Maya>After steps three, four and five, they are warm, so you get the best result.

93
00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.940
<v Dev>Is there an analogy for the importance of order?

94
00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.150
<v Maya>Making tea.

95
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.960
<v Maya>If you put ginger and cardamom in first, then tea, and add water and milk only at the end, the tea won't turn out right.

96
00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:01.230
<v Maya>Even a routine activity needs the correct order.

97
00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:04.220
<v Dev>A student asked, do we test mobility too?

98
00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:11.050
<v Maya>Mobility is the ability of joints to move freely and actively through a full range of motion without discomfort.

99
00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:19.510
<v Maya>It goes hand in hand with flexibility, and some flexibility tests check range of motion at a joint, so mobility is tested side by side.

100
00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.820
<v Maya>It is not listed separately because there is no component named mobility.

101
00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:24.990
<v Maya>Quick recap.

102
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.160
<v Maya>Order is blood pressure, body composition, cardio, strength, endurance, flexibility.

103
00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:36.470
<v Maya>Reasons: health marker, neutral body, heart rate purity, fresh muscles, priming effect, warm tissues.

104
00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.020
<v Dev>Pause and think, then.

105
00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:42.260
<v Dev>Why is it wrong to put the push-up test before the bench press test?

106
00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.690
<v Maya>Go on, Dev.

107
00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:44.850
<v Dev>Fatigue.

108
00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:49.570
<v Dev>The chest would be tired, so the maximum force would be underestimated.

109
00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:50.310
<v Maya>Correct.

110
00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.080
<v Maya>Now blood pressure itself.

111
00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:56.890
<v Maya>A reading looks like one hundred and fifteen over seventy five millimetres of mercury.

112
00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.620
<v Maya>People call them the upper one and the lower one.

113
00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.160
<v Maya>The professional terms are systolic and diastolic.

114
00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.630
<v Dev>Which is which?

115
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.100
<v Maya>Systolic is the upper, larger number.

116
00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:11.940
<v Maya>When the heart beats and the left ventricle pumps blood into the vessels, pressure is highest.

117
00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:14.130
<v Maya>Diastolic is the lower number.

118
00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:17.820
<v Maya>In the gap between two heartbeats, pressure falls to its lowest.

119
00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.230
<v Dev>What's normal?

120
00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.510
<v Maya>Systolic ninety to one hundred and twenty, diastolic sixty to eighty.

121
00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:29.730
<v Maya>The unit, m m H g, is millimetres of mercury, and Hg is the chemical symbol for mercury.

122
00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:35.110
<v Maya>A reading of one fifteen over seventy five is inside both ranges, so it's normal.

123
00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.780
<v Dev>What's the equipment?

124
00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:43.590
<v Maya>Older manual equipment has a balloon, a cuff, a column of mercury called a sphygmomanometer, and a stethoscope.

125
00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:44.920
<v Maya>You don't need that.

126
00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.700
<v Maya>You use a simple automatic digital monitor.

127
00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:53.990
<v Maya>The tube from the cuff is plugged in, the cuff goes on the upper arm, you press start and the machine does everything.

128
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:59.540
<v Maya>The display shows SYS for systolic, DIA for diastolic and the pulse rate.

129
00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.890
<v Dev>The cuff procedure sounds fiddly.

130
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.160
<v Maya>Seven steps.

131
00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.420
<v Maya>One, plug the tube into the device.

132
00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:09.850
<v Maya>Two, tie the cuff on the upper arm with both the tube and the palm facing up.

133
00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:15.040
<v Maya>Three, put the cuff edge two to three centimetres above the elbow, so it doesn't cover the joint.

134
00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:18.500
<v Maya>Four, snug but not too tight, two fingers should fit under it.

135
00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:22.010
<v Maya>Five, the tube passes along the medial side of the arm.

136
00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:26.670
<v Maya>Six, the cuff is at the same height as the heart, so use a table or armrest.

137
00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:33.510
<v Maya>Seven, posture: legs not crossed, feet flat, back supported, and no talking or stress, from client or tester.

138
00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.850
<v Dev>Medial means the inner side, towards the body.

139
00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:37.750
<v Maya>Yes.

140
00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.350
<v Maya>Now classification.

141
00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:45.620
<v Maya>Low blood pressure, called hypotension, is systolic below ninety or diastolic below sixty.

142
00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:47.040
<v Maya>Either one is enough.

143
00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.370
<v Maya>Normal is ninety to one twenty and sixty to eighty.

144
00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:54.830
<v Maya>Elevated is one twenty to one twenty nine, and diastolic below eighty.

145
00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:00.090
<v Maya>Hypertension stage one is one thirty to one thirty nine, or eighty to eighty nine.

146
00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:03.510
<v Maya>Stage two is one forty or higher, or ninety or higher.

147
00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:12.450
<v Maya>A hypertensive crisis is systolic one eighty or higher, or diastolic higher than one twenty, or both, and needs a doctor immediately.

148
00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.160
<v Dev>Hypo and hyper?

149
00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.970
<v Maya>Hypo means less, hyper means excessive.

150
00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:24.430
<v Maya>And the safety link: if a client already had stage one and activity pushed them to stage two or a crisis, it would be very risky.

151
00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.060
<v Maya>That's why blood pressure is checked first.

152
00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.850
<v Dev>So I don't need every cell, just the pattern.

153
00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:35.990
<v Dev>Low is under ninety or sixty, normal ninety to twenty, sixty to eighty, then it climbs.

154
00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:44.300
<v Maya>One note for your course: the slide boundaries overlap slightly, for example one twenty appears in normal and elevated, so follow your slide chart.

155
00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.470
<v Maya>Let's close.

156
00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.960
<v Maya>We learned the order and the reasons, and the blood pressure test.

157
00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:54.780
<v Dev>The order is blood pressure, body composition, cardio, strength, endurance, flexibility.

158
00:08:54.830 --> 00:09:00.600
<v Dev>And blood pressure is systolic upper, diastolic lower, with a seven step cuff routine.

159
00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:02.270
<v Maya>Five things to remember.

160
00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.420
<v Maya>One, the order is not a rank, it protects accuracy.

161
00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:11.640
<v Maya>Two, if blood pressure is high, rest, re-check, and try for up to three days, then refer to a doctor.

162
00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:15.960
<v Maya>Three, strength before endurance because of fatigue and the priming effect.

163
00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:21.780
<v Maya>Four, normal blood pressure is systolic ninety to one twenty and diastolic sixty to eighty.

164
00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:27.550
<v Maya>Five, the cuff goes two to three centimetres above the elbow, snug with two fingers, at heart height.

165
00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.770
<v Dev>Next, body composition.

166
00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.060
<v Maya>See you in part three.

